1
00:00:08,317 --> 00:00:09,119
Good evening.

2
00:00:09,320 --> 00:00:11,811
I trust you had a fruitful day.

3
00:00:11,830 --> 00:00:15,205
I do not say happy, though I hope it has been happy.

4
00:00:15,961 --> 00:00:45,698
Namo tassa bhagavato arahato sambha sambhudassa Namo tassa bhagavato arahato sambha sambhudassa Namo tassa bhagavato arahato sambha sambhudassa Homage to the Buddha, the Blessed Noble and fully Self-Awakened One.

5
00:00:49,728 --> 00:00:57,036
So this evening, we're moving to another character called Potaliya.

6
00:00:57,915 --> 00:00:59,497
He's Majima Kukai number 54.

7
00:01:01,420 --> 00:01:04,823
So again, we have a very well-known scene.

8
00:01:04,843 --> 00:01:07,686
The Blessed One has taken his outer robe.

9
00:01:07,746 --> 00:01:11,170
He's gone to the local capital, Arpana.

10
00:01:11,189 --> 00:01:17,777
This is in the Anguttara country, which I think is south of the Ganges, not sure.

11
00:01:18,870 --> 00:01:21,715
And he then went to a certain grove.

12
00:01:21,754 --> 00:01:24,819
So we don't know the name of this particular grove.

13
00:01:24,920 --> 00:01:27,102
Having entered the grove, he sat down at the root of a tree.

14
00:01:27,522 --> 00:01:35,795
Now Portilia, a householder, was walking and wandering for exercise, wearing full dress with parasol and sandals.

15
00:01:36,456 --> 00:01:42,284
He also went to the grove and went to the Blessed One, exchanged greetings with him.

16
00:01:43,525 --> 00:01:44,968
There are seats, householder.

17
00:01:45,028 --> 00:01:46,971
Sit down as you like, said the Blessed One.

18
00:01:47,778 --> 00:01:52,603
And the householder thought, hmm, this recluse Gautama addresses me as a householder.

19
00:01:53,364 --> 00:01:56,888
And angry, he was angry and displeased and remained silent.

20
00:01:58,010 --> 00:02:02,155
So the Buddha again invited him, please, householder, sit as you would like.

21
00:02:02,435 --> 00:02:03,697
And the third time.

22
00:02:04,537 --> 00:02:13,048
And then the third time when he said, sit down as you like, Potalia was still displeased because he was being addressed as a householder.

23
00:02:13,588 --> 00:02:15,591
And so he said, Master Gautama,

24
00:02:15,907 --> 00:02:20,375
It is neither fitting nor proper that you address me as a householder.

25
00:02:21,796 --> 00:02:22,578
A householder?

26
00:02:22,959 --> 00:02:26,264
You have all the aspects, the marks and signs of a householder.

27
00:02:26,824 --> 00:02:34,676
After all, he's walking and wandering for exercise, wearing full dress with parasol and sandals.

28
00:02:36,748 --> 00:02:41,835
Nevertheless, Master Gautama, I have given up all my works and cut off my affairs.

29
00:02:42,276 --> 00:02:55,431
So here, we have to remember, whenever you get this word householder, we're talking about people who are still caught up in the sensual life, who aren't really practicing spiritually, which means following the Buddha, right?

30
00:02:55,451 --> 00:03:02,661
They may be following other leaders, but the Buddha may not recognize that as a truly spiritual life.

31
00:03:02,760 --> 00:03:03,481
I don't know.

32
00:03:03,800 --> 00:03:16,332
So you always get this word Bhikkhu, which refers to anybody who's following the spiritual life, according to the Buddha, and a householder who isn't.

33
00:03:17,533 --> 00:03:25,822
So this potter is a little bit annoyed that he'd been called a householder when he's given up everything, you see.

34
00:03:26,522 --> 00:03:32,389
And in what way, asks the Buddha, have you given up all your works, householder, cut off all your affairs?

35
00:03:32,977 --> 00:03:40,805
Master Gautama, I've given up my wealth, grain, silver and gold, he's a rich fellow, to my children as their inheritance.

36
00:03:41,224 --> 00:03:46,810
Without advising or admonishing them, I live merely on food and clothing.

37
00:03:46,909 --> 00:03:51,235
This is how I've given up my works and cut off all my affairs.

38
00:03:51,895 --> 00:04:02,004
So on the outer, looking at it from a particular angle, he's now just living on food and clothing, just like anybody who has gone out

39
00:04:02,288 --> 00:04:05,131
and left the household life and become a samana.

40
00:04:05,151 --> 00:04:12,777
So a samana is an ascetic, well, a wanderer.

41
00:04:12,798 --> 00:04:24,528
So the Buddha replies, as you describe it is one thing, but in the noble one's discipline, that's his own discipline, the cutting off of affairs is different.

42
00:04:25,629 --> 00:04:30,374
What is the cutting off of affairs like in the noble one's dispensation, Venerable Sir?

43
00:04:30,855 --> 00:04:32,076
It'd be good to know this.

44
00:04:32,730 --> 00:04:34,394
So listen carefully, Householder.

45
00:04:34,995 --> 00:04:36,317
Yes, Venerable Sir.

46
00:04:36,338 --> 00:04:37,942
So he's got a bit more humble, I think.

47
00:04:40,105 --> 00:04:44,735
So there are eight things that lead to cutting off of affairs.

48
00:04:44,776 --> 00:04:47,762
So these are the eight abandonments.

49
00:04:50,206 --> 00:04:53,894
With the support of non-killing of living beings,

50
00:04:54,112 --> 00:04:57,435
The killing of living beings is abandoned.

51
00:04:57,617 --> 00:05:05,906
So this is a very roundabout way of saying giving up killing living beings, you then have abandoned killing living beings.

52
00:05:08,471 --> 00:05:13,216
The scriptures have a sort of way of repeating themselves, remember, so they can be remembered.

53
00:05:14,581 --> 00:05:17,925
And this is true also of false speech, right?

54
00:05:17,944 --> 00:05:22,711
Giving up, abandoning false speech, abandoning malicious speech.

55
00:05:22,771 --> 00:05:24,634
So many of these we've come across, of course.

56
00:05:25,555 --> 00:05:27,598
Abandoning rapacious greed.

57
00:05:27,677 --> 00:05:33,286
I don't quite know why the word rapacious is put on greed, but it's obviously rapacious greed.

58
00:05:33,326 --> 00:05:38,833
So it's really heavy greed to the detriment of oneself and others, etc.

59
00:05:39,437 --> 00:06:07,048
spiteful scolding spiteful scolding um in in the notes it talks about violent reprisal so that's a bit more than violent scolding that's the commentaries uh an angry despair angry despair is to be abandoned uh it doesn't support why why we should be angry and despair or the despair of anger or the the anger of despair whatever

60
00:06:07,754 --> 00:06:14,302
With the support of, sorry, and without arrogance needs to be abandoned.

61
00:06:14,322 --> 00:06:26,721
So obviously this potilia, he has a little large dose of arrogance, walking around as if he were living the true spiritual life, when in fact he's actually quite comfortable.

62
00:06:28,043 --> 00:06:34,351
These are the eight things stated in brief, which are to be expounded in detail that lead to the cutting off of affairs.

63
00:06:34,720 --> 00:06:52,057
So we have the usual non-killing, false speech, lying, malicious speech, rapacious greed, spiteful scolding, which also can be violent, it seems, angry despair, and arrogance.

64
00:06:52,076 --> 00:06:55,199
I've missed one.

65
00:06:55,220 --> 00:06:55,661
Try again.

66
00:06:56,341 --> 00:07:02,887
The killing of living beings, taking what is not freely given, false speech, ah yes, taking what is not freely given.

67
00:07:03,322 --> 00:07:10,851
false speech, malicious speech, rapacious greed, spiteful scolding, angry despair, and arrogance.

68
00:07:10,872 --> 00:07:13,035
So these are the eight abandonments.

69
00:07:13,915 --> 00:07:18,461
So now Potilia wants him to expound on these, right?

70
00:07:18,482 --> 00:07:21,706
So he says, here a noble disciple considers this.

71
00:07:22,687 --> 00:07:31,237
I am practicing the way to the abandoning and cutting off of those fetters because of which I might kill living beings.

72
00:07:32,483 --> 00:07:42,115
If I were to kill living beings, so these are reflections that one has if one were to do these unwholesome actions.

73
00:07:43,016 --> 00:07:47,041
If I were to kill living beings, I would blame myself for doing so.

74
00:07:48,884 --> 00:07:52,889
The wise, having investigated, would censure me for doing so.

75
00:07:54,031 --> 00:08:00,279
On the dissolution of the body after death because of killing living beings, an unhappy destination.

76
00:08:00,596 --> 00:08:02,800
could be expected, would be expected.

77
00:08:04,343 --> 00:08:09,471
And the killing of living beings itself is a fetter and then a hindrance.

78
00:08:09,492 --> 00:08:14,581
So the fetter, in other words, ties you back into this form of existence.

79
00:08:15,100 --> 00:08:18,387
And of course, in our meditation, turns up as a hindrance.

80
00:08:18,552 --> 00:08:22,800
a hindrance of self-blaming, of guilt, et cetera, et cetera.

81
00:08:22,821 --> 00:08:23,382
Okay?

82
00:08:23,401 --> 00:08:24,545
So these are the things.

83
00:08:25,807 --> 00:08:36,308
If we did these unwholesome acts, we behaved in this unwholesome way, then we would end up just blaming ourselves, feeling bad about ourselves.

84
00:08:36,590 --> 00:08:36,931
Shame.

85
00:08:37,150 --> 00:08:38,113
That's shame, isn't it?

86
00:08:38,582 --> 00:08:43,029
We have other people telling us how awful we were in doing so.

87
00:08:44,091 --> 00:08:49,759
On the dissolution of the body after death, we could end up in a very unhappy destination.

88
00:08:51,062 --> 00:08:58,793
And, of course, it becomes a fetter for us, this killing, cutting us off from the awakening, you see.

89
00:08:59,374 --> 00:09:06,345
Although it's not an actual fetter, capital F, it still remains as something that ties us down

90
00:09:06,950 --> 00:09:10,674
and, of course, a hindrance in our practice.

91
00:09:10,695 --> 00:09:28,738
And he says this, of course, for the rest of them, which, remember, is taking what is not freely given, false speech, malicious speech, rapacious greed, spiteful scolding, and angry despair, and arrogance, of course, arrogance.

92
00:09:29,719 --> 00:09:36,788
Then, having said this, these eight things that lead to the cutting off of affairs in the noble one's disciple

93
00:09:36,937 --> 00:09:38,818
have now been expounded in detail.

94
00:09:39,178 --> 00:09:47,307
But the cutting off of affairs in the noble one's disciples has not been yet entirely and achieved entirely in all ways.

95
00:09:47,889 --> 00:09:49,090
So it's what the Buddha says to him.

96
00:09:49,509 --> 00:09:51,011
Well, how is that done?

97
00:09:51,111 --> 00:09:56,357
How do you cut off everything so that you become fully liberated?

98
00:09:56,798 --> 00:09:57,558
So he says, listen.

99
00:09:57,958 --> 00:10:03,264
And then he comes, and the Buddha gives four or five, I think, different metaphors.

100
00:10:03,548 --> 00:10:05,491
So the Buddha loves to give metaphors.

101
00:10:05,552 --> 00:10:17,913
I think he would have been something like a scriptwriter or a novelist or something, because he's always having stated the thing in a sort of logical, rational way.

102
00:10:18,195 --> 00:10:20,118
He then loves to give these metaphors.

103
00:10:20,639 --> 00:10:21,541
So here's the first one.

104
00:10:23,124 --> 00:10:27,631
Suppose a dog overcome by hunger and weakness was waiting by a butcher's shop.

105
00:10:28,388 --> 00:10:35,059
Then a skilled butcher or apprentice would cut out the skeleton of meatless bones smeared with blood and toss it to the dog.

106
00:10:35,519 --> 00:10:36,601
What do you think, householder?

107
00:10:36,863 --> 00:10:43,514
Would that dog get rid of his hunger and weakness by gnawing such a skeleton of meatless bones smeared with blood?

108
00:10:44,174 --> 00:10:45,037
No, venerable sir.

109
00:10:45,658 --> 00:10:48,582
Eventually that dog would reap weariness and disappointment.

110
00:10:50,606 --> 00:10:53,772
So there's the image of greed or whatever.

111
00:10:54,950 --> 00:11:23,860
uh so and then he says uh sensual pleasure is compared to the skeleton by the blessed one etc and uh he having seen this with proper wisdom seeing the problem with indulgence with proper wisdom he avoids the equanimity that is diversified based on diversity and develops the equanimity that is unified based on unity now

112
00:11:24,193 --> 00:11:36,109
Equanimity that is diversified and based on diversity is that sort of boredom that comes up or the sense of indifference or a sense of apathy.

113
00:11:36,769 --> 00:11:42,216
Perhaps that's the best, a sense of apathy that comes up because things are no longer pleasing you.

114
00:11:42,716 --> 00:11:45,419
And the diversity, of course, is variety, right?

115
00:11:45,440 --> 00:11:52,428
Variety is the spice of life, but it also leads ultimately to this apathy, to this boredom.

116
00:11:52,813 --> 00:11:59,063
Whereas equanimity, which is unified, this is always the equanimity that comes with concentration.

117
00:11:59,565 --> 00:12:03,831
Here specifically, he's talking about these absorption states.

118
00:12:04,513 --> 00:12:11,403
But just in our practice, in order of daily life, we can end up, we can experience an equanimous mind.

119
00:12:12,866 --> 00:12:14,889
And when it's equanimous, you're sure it's not clinging.

120
00:12:14,909 --> 00:12:15,530
So that's the point.

121
00:12:17,182 --> 00:12:24,695
Then he talks about a vulture or a crow or a hawk sees a piece of meat and sees the piece of meat and flew away.

122
00:12:24,995 --> 00:12:29,744
Then vultures, crows and hawks flew up and pecked and clawed it.

123
00:12:30,183 --> 00:12:31,206
What do you think, householder?

124
00:12:31,746 --> 00:12:39,240
If that vulture, crow or hawk does not quickly let go of the piece of meat, wouldn't it incur death or deadly suffering because of that?

125
00:12:39,793 --> 00:12:41,015
Indeed, it would, you see.

126
00:12:41,395 --> 00:12:52,089
So the best one said, well, they provide much suffering and much despair, while the danger in them is great.

127
00:12:52,190 --> 00:12:54,572
So this, of course, again, is sensual pleasures.

128
00:12:54,592 --> 00:12:56,715
Sensual pleasures just compared to a piece of meat.

129
00:12:58,697 --> 00:13:00,840
Then again, we have the blazing grass torch.

130
00:13:01,541 --> 00:13:05,687
And a man took a blazing grass torch and went against the wind.

131
00:13:05,746 --> 00:13:06,847
What do you think, householder?

132
00:13:07,047 --> 00:13:15,139
If that man does not quickly let go of that blazing grass torch, wouldn't that blazing grass torch burn his hand and his arm or some other parts of the body?

133
00:13:15,479 --> 00:13:18,744
He might incur death or deadly suffering because of that.

134
00:13:19,144 --> 00:13:19,804
Yes, indeed.

135
00:13:19,946 --> 00:13:22,649
So this is compared.

136
00:13:22,690 --> 00:13:28,197
This is, again, sensual pleasure and the usual thing about suffering, much despair, etc.

137
00:13:28,839 --> 00:13:30,301
Then he's got the charcoal pit.

138
00:13:30,421 --> 00:13:31,042
There's a great one.

139
00:13:31,081 --> 00:13:36,750
There's deeper than a man's height, full of glowing coals without flame or smoke.

140
00:13:37,303 --> 00:13:49,000
Then a man who wanted to live and not to die, who wanted pleasure and recoiled from pain, and two strong men seized him by both arms and dragged him towards the charcoal pit.

141
00:13:49,361 --> 00:13:50,423
What do you think, householder?

142
00:13:50,844 --> 00:13:53,427
Would that man not twist his body this way and that?

143
00:13:54,589 --> 00:14:00,597
Definitely, Venerables, definitely, for he will incur death or deadly suffering because of that.

144
00:14:03,162 --> 00:14:06,687
So again, sensual pleasures is compared to a charcoal pit.

145
00:14:07,509 --> 00:14:08,731
Then there's this one.

146
00:14:08,932 --> 00:14:13,980
A man dreamt about lovely parks, lovely groves, lovely meadows and lovely lakes.

147
00:14:14,559 --> 00:14:17,303
And on walking out, he saw nothing of it.

148
00:14:18,605 --> 00:14:24,355
So, too, a noble disciple considers sensual pleasures have to be compared to a dream.

149
00:14:25,697 --> 00:14:28,200
They provide much suffering and much despair.

150
00:14:28,821 --> 00:14:29,282
So that's it.

151
00:14:29,633 --> 00:14:44,249
Then another one, a man borrowed goods on loan, a fancy carriage and fine jeweled earrings, and proceeded and surrounded by these borrowed goods, he went to the market.

152
00:14:44,769 --> 00:14:48,432
Then people seeing him would say, sirs, this is a rich man.

153
00:14:49,033 --> 00:14:51,517
This is how the rich enjoy their wealth.

154
00:14:52,337 --> 00:14:56,282
Then the owners, when they saw him, would take their things back.

155
00:14:56,784 --> 00:14:58,067
What do you think, Householder?

156
00:14:58,368 --> 00:15:01,692
Would that be enough for that man to become dejected, depressed?

157
00:15:02,073 --> 00:15:03,436
Definitely.

158
00:15:04,157 --> 00:15:06,061
This is the problem with sensual pleasure.

159
00:15:06,741 --> 00:15:08,043
Things are on loan.

160
00:15:08,124 --> 00:15:09,687
They don't belong to us.

161
00:15:12,490 --> 00:15:14,475
And then there's another one.

162
00:15:15,515 --> 00:15:16,498
Got quite a few, actually.

163
00:15:18,418 --> 00:15:45,625
uh suppose there was a dense grove not far from the village or town within which there was a tree laden with fruit but none of its fruit had fallen to the ground then a man came needing fruit seeking fruit wandering in search of fruit and he entered the grove and saw the tree laden with fruit and he thought to himself this tree is laden with fruit but as none none has fallen the ground i know how to climb a tree i shall climb the tree eat as much fruit as i want and fill my bag

164
00:15:46,432 --> 00:15:47,092
And he did so.

165
00:15:47,573 --> 00:15:53,024
Then a second man, needing to seek fruit much the same way, came with a sharp axe.

166
00:15:53,966 --> 00:15:56,511
This tree is laden with fruit, he thought to himself.

167
00:15:57,033 --> 00:16:01,841
I do not know how to climb, so I'll cut the tree down, eat as much as I want and fill my bag.

168
00:16:02,446 --> 00:16:16,263
What do you think, Householder, if that first man who had climbed the tree doesn't come down quickly when the tree falls, wouldn't he break a hand or a foot or some other part of the body so that he might incur death or deadly suffering because of that?

169
00:16:16,702 --> 00:16:17,945
Indeed, Venerable Sir.

170
00:16:19,726 --> 00:16:22,009
So this again is the problem with sensual pleasures.

171
00:16:23,191 --> 00:16:30,580
Having seen this thus and actually with proper wisdom, he avoids the equanimity that is diversified based on diversity.

172
00:16:31,032 --> 00:16:40,065
So he avoids the consequence of pleasure, which is to get bored, to become apathetic, etc.

173
00:16:40,525 --> 00:16:43,330
And he develops the equanimity, which is based on meditation.

174
00:16:45,913 --> 00:16:53,184
Now, when he does that, he can eventually, of course, in the fullness of time, he moves towards full liberation.

175
00:16:53,985 --> 00:17:00,515
And in that way, he can begin to see how he has ended up where he has, which is to see past lives.

176
00:17:00,866 --> 00:17:12,632
Because of the divine eye, this clairvoyance, you can see that other beings are also moving from one realm to the next due to their ethical behavior.

177
00:17:13,220 --> 00:17:33,589
And then finally, at that same supreme mindfulness whose purity is beyond, is due to equanimity, realizing for himself with direct knowledge, the disciple here and now enters upon and abides in the deliverance of mind and the deliverance by wisdom that are taintless with the destruction of the old taints.

178
00:17:33,609 --> 00:17:35,311
Remember the old taints, the usings.

179
00:17:36,472 --> 00:17:43,779
Deliverance of mind is to do with the jhana, which in pure vipassana, of course, is also when all the hindrances disappear.

180
00:17:44,480 --> 00:17:47,082
And deliverance of wisdom, of course, is insight.

181
00:17:48,523 --> 00:17:54,328
Then there's a final paragraph where this potaliya talks about thoroughbreds.

182
00:17:54,769 --> 00:18:04,438
So he says, up until now, all these monks that came from monastics, that came from other teachers,

183
00:18:04,789 --> 00:18:07,173
we imagine them to be thoroughbreds, right?

184
00:18:08,114 --> 00:18:09,236
The real thing, you see.

185
00:18:10,017 --> 00:18:18,788
And therefore they got food and were seated at the place and given proper respect, you see.

186
00:18:19,068 --> 00:18:30,984
But because he and his family thought that the bhikkhus of the Buddha were not thoroughbreds, then of course they got inferior food and were given lower places.

187
00:18:31,046 --> 00:18:33,648
But now that will completely turn around.

188
00:18:33,898 --> 00:18:40,186
because he's been convinced by the Buddha that this is the true path.

189
00:18:40,207 --> 00:18:47,414
And so the final paragraph is one that we see repeated throughout all the scriptures when somebody does that.

190
00:18:48,115 --> 00:18:52,101
Magnificent Master Gautama, magnificent Master Gautama.

191
00:18:52,601 --> 00:19:03,213
Master Gautama has made the Dharma clear in many ways, as though he were turning upright what had been overturned, revealing what was hidden.

192
00:19:03,446 --> 00:19:10,115
showing the way to one who was lost, holding up a lamp in the dark for those who can see.

193
00:19:10,856 --> 00:19:15,242
I go to Master Gautama for refuge, to the Dharma, to the Sangha.

194
00:19:15,963 --> 00:19:23,054
From today, let Master Gautama remember me as a lay follower who has gone to him for refuge for life.

195
00:19:23,454 --> 00:19:29,143
So here we have another convert to the Buddha's teaching.

196
00:19:29,163 --> 00:19:32,086
And so it's just for us really to...

197
00:19:32,776 --> 00:19:36,760
contemplate our lives, to remember these eight abandonments.

198
00:19:39,365 --> 00:19:51,160
I'll just remind you again of this lovely little book, Pressing Out Pure Honey, you can't read it, Pressing Out Pure Honey by Sharda Rajel, in which she's gone through Om Ujjima Nikaya and made little notes.

199
00:19:51,319 --> 00:19:59,069
So here, for instance, in this particular discourse, she tells us a little bit of opening, and then she tells us these eight things, killing, stealing,

200
00:20:01,260 --> 00:20:22,474
greed, spiteful scolding, angry despair and arrogance and that if you do these things then of course you end up blaming yourself, being rebuked by the wise, end up in unhappy destinations and then of course the rising of the taints, the usings and with it vexation and fever.

201
